WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 10 Student Video
Unit 3, Lesson 2: Universe

1
00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:05.290
Imagine you go for a hike and come across a cabin.

2
00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:08.630
It looks interesting, so you decide to explore.

3
00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:13.270
But as you grow closer, you notice something strange.

4
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.950
You hear the sound of your favorite song in the background.

5
00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:24.310
When you open the door, you see shoes and a jacket fitted perfectly for you.

6
00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.860
On the table, you see the books and magazines you like to read.

7
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:33.580
In

8
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.440
the refrigerator, you notice your favorite snacks.

9
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:39.260
And

10
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.320
in the bathroom, you find all the toiletries you normally use.

11
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.680
Everything you need to survive, tailored just to your liking, is there.

12
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:54.360
How

13
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.100
did the cabin get arranged so precisely as it is?

14
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:08.240
Maybe you just got lucky, and all of this is a matter of coincidence.

15
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.700
But that doesn't seem very reasonable if you think about it for a minute, does it?

16
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.180
I mean, there's just too many things tailored precisely for you.

17
00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:22.700
You might not know why, or you might not know who did it, but clearly there's some

18
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.620
purpose and design behind the arrangement of this cabin.

19
00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:32.880
Freeman J. Dyson, a renowned theoretical physicist, once said, As we look out

20
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.900
into the universe and identify the many accidents of physics and astronomy that have

21
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:43.360
worked to our benefit, it almost seems as if the universe must in some sense have

22
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.100
known that we were coming. The laws of physics and astronomy

23
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:53.360
all exist within a very small range, to allow the universe to support

24
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:59.160
life. It's as if these laws exist on a razor's edge, and the slightest change

25
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:04.900
to the left or the right, and the entire universe becomes inhospitable to life.

26
00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:09.980
When I was in college, some friends of mine lived on the third story of an apartment

27
00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:14.640
building with a window, overlooking a parking lot, tall row of trees, a big water

28
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.540
gutter, and then a stop sign. And they thought it'd be

29
00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:22.260
entertaining and funny to open up their window, use one of those huge water balloon

30
00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.200
launchers, and launch balloons to try to hit cars as they came home.

31
00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:30.300
Well, one evening we were sitting there, and we launched one from the window.

32
00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.620
It cleared the water gutter, the trees, the parking lot, and hit a car right on the

33
00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:40.860
windshield, and cracked it. And yes, we had to pay for it.

34
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.160
Imagine how many factors we had to get right to hit our target.

35
00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.120
For example, what if there was more water in the balloon?

36
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:49.580
We wouldn't have gone far enough.

37
00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.200
What if there was less water? We would have gone too far.

38
00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.440
What if we were up in the mountains where there's different air pressure?

39
00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.680
We would have gone further. What if the tension in the launcher was different?

40
00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.020
There's all these things we had to get right to hit our target.

41
00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.380
Well, that's not too different from our universe as a whole.

42
00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.620
You see, the universe has been expanding at a very carefully and calibrated rate

43
00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:17.180
since its inception. But if that rate were different, in comparison with the mass

44
00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.920
density, or with the dark energy, then we couldn't even have a universe that's

45
00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:27.680
capable of supporting life. So what are the mathematical odds that a

46
00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:33.220
universe, with its laws of physics and cosmology randomly generated, being

47
00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:37.000
capable of having stars which are necessary for there to be life?

48
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:43.580
Well, according to astrophysicist Lee Smolin, the odds are 1 times 10 to the 229th

49
00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:46.200
power. You realize how big of a number that is?

50
00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.280
That's one, with 229 zeros after it.

51
00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:55.840
Essentially, that's equivalent to taking every single proton in the entire universe.

52
00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:01.960
And each of those protons would represent its own universe with laws of physics

53
00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:07.640
randomly generated. Mathematically speaking, if that were the case, none of these

54
00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.800
universes would be capable of generating stars, which means it wouldn't be capable

55
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:16.300
of having life. But let's compare this to our cabin.

56
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:21.520
What if you were blindfolded in this cabin and you were trying to find a lost coin?

57
00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:23.660
What are the odds that you could find it?

58
00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.560
Well, what if we expanded it to the city that I'm in?

59
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:31.380
Or to the country? Or to the entire planet?

60
00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.620
Or how about our solar system? What about our galaxy?

61
00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:38.380
What if we expanded it to the entire universe?

62
00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:43.200
What are the mathematical odds that blindfolded you could find this coin?

63
00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:48.980
Do you realize that it is far more likely, far more likely, you would find this coin

64
00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:54.440
than we would find ourselves in a universe with randomly generated laws capable

65
00:04:54.440 --> 00:05:00.420
of supporting complex life? There is very little debate that our universe is

66
00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:06.300
exquisitely fine-tuned. The question is, what best explains the fine-tuning?

67
00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:11.080
One option people often say is chance, but I think we've seen clearly

68
00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:14.940
mathematically that this is not a reasonable explanation.

69
00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.620
Another option people say is law.

70
00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:23.780
Maybe there's some law that requires the other laws to be fine-tuned for life.

71
00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:29.200
But the problem is, is that laws in physics and cosmology tend to create things that

72
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:35.060
are simple and repeatable, not complex and specified like we see in the laws that

73
00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:40.680
govern our universe. One final option often used to explain away fine-tuning is

74
00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:45.540
called the multiverse, which is the idea that either there's a ton of universes out

75
00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.540
there, or an infinite number of universes.

76
00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:52.440
Therefore, we shouldn't be surprised that we happen to find ourselves in a universe

77
00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:56.880
fine-tuned for life. Well, there's a couple problems with appealing to this to

78
00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.420
explain away fine-tuning. One problem is that there's no direct scientific evidence

79
00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:06.520
for the multiverse. At this stage, it's scientific conjecture.

80
00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.800
But even if we do find evidence for the multiverse, this wouldn't explain away

81
00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:16.620
design. In fact, all it would do is move design away from this universe to the

82
00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:20.380
multiverse. There would still need to be an explanation for why there is a

83
00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:24.940
multiverse that generates individually fine-tuned universes.

84
00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.380
So think about it this way. If we're trying to explain away design of a car, and

85
00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:32.420
somebody says, oh, we can explain away by a factory, you'd say, wait a minute.

86
00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:36.200
There still needs to be a sufficient explanation for the factory.

87
00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:42.840
Rather than law, chance, or the multiverse explaining away fine-tuning, I

88
00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:48.340
think the best explanation is that there is a fine-tuner who organized the universe

89
00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:53.960
with us in mind. Just as the arrangement of the cabin pointed towards somebody

90
00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:59.680
expecting our arrival, the exquisite fine-tuning of life best

91
00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:05.280
points towards someone expecting us to live here and even to feel at home.

